{
  "title": "The ovarian cycle and the endometrium",
  "sources": [
    "CH24S31",
    "CH24S32",
    "CH24S33",
    "CH24S34",
    "CH24S35",
    "CH24S36"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Follicular growth",
      "text": "Follow the ovary and the uterine lining together. Rising follicle-stimulating hormone stimulates follicular growth. One follicle becomes dominant and produces increasing estradiol. Estrogen drives proliferation of the endometrium."
    },
    {
      "start": 14.5,
      "title": "Ovulation",
      "text": "The luteinizing hormone surge causes ovulation. Watch the mature follicle release the ovum. Then follow the remaining follicle as it becomes the corpus luteum."
    },
    {
      "start": 24.66,
      "title": "Secretory phase",
      "text": "Luteinizing hormone stimulates the corpus luteum to secrete progesterone. Progesterone initiates the secretory phase. Endometrial glands and blood vessels branch and curl through the functional layer."
    },
    {
      "start": 36.46,
      "title": "Without implantation",
      "text": "If implantation does not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates. Estrogen and progesterone fall, and the endometrium degenerates and sheds. The hormone sequence explains the tissue sequence: follicular growth, ovulation, secretory change, and menstruation. Estrogen supports proliferation; progesterone initiates secretion."
    }
  ],
